  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 107 AM EST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nUpper ridge extends into the Great Lakes region from the sw today, \nand will only slowly ease downstream midweek. An ejecting shortwave \nwill run into and somewhat flatten this ridge Thursday, but \nridging aloft is quickly restored in the wake of that system \nFriday, and ahead of broad troffing in the west. \n\nAt the surface, high pressure over OH will drift toward VA this \nweek. Plenty of dry air emanating off of this high, so despite the \nlengthy period of deep southerly flow that only just beginning, it \nwill be difficult for us to moisten. Surface low track Thursday is \nfrom nw MN to James Bay, so well well to our north. Strongest \nforcing is also to our north, though DPVA forcing is respectable as \nfar south as Lk Superior. And so the best shower chances will be \nnorth, though perhaps the eastern UP has a shot at a stray shower \nThursday.\n\nAn associated weak cold front and trailing surface high pressure \nwill cool us off 3-5F Friday, though still solidly above \nnormal. Then strong warm advection heading into Saturday, ahead \nof the next system ejecting from the west. Saturday still looks \nlike the day where could perhaps take a run at some record \nhighs. Then strong low pressure passes to our north, sending a \nstronger cold front across northern MI Sunday. By Monday \nforecast max temps are close to normal. Best rain chances are \nSat night- Sunday, with a chance for rain and snow showers \nMonday.\n\nBreezy each day thru Thursday, with Thursday probably this windiest. \nAlso windy at times Saturday into Sunday night.",
